## Proof

We evaluate
$$S_n = \sum_{k=1}^n \frac{2^{f(k)}+2^{-f(k)}}{2^n}, \qquad f(k)=\left\lfloor \sqrt{k}+\tfrac12\right\rfloor.$$

**Key observation.** Since $f(k) = \lfloor \sqrt{k}+1/2\rfloor \le \sqrt{k}+\tfrac12$, for every $k\le n$:
$$f(k) \le \sqrt{k}+\tfrac12 \le \sqrt{n}+\tfrac12.$$

Also, for $k\ge 1$: $f(k) \ge \lfloor 1+\tfrac12\rfloor = 1 > 0$, so $2^{-f(k)} \le 1$.

**Upper bound.** Each summand satisfies
$$2^{f(k)}+2^{-f(k)} \le 2^{\sqrt{n}+1/2}+1.$$

Summing over $k=1,\dots,n$ and dividing by $2^n$:
$$0 \le S_n \le \frac{n\left(2^{\sqrt{n}+1/2}+1\right)}{2^n} = n\cdot 2^{\sqrt{n}+1/2-n}+\frac{n}{2^n}.$$

**Both terms vanish.** As $n\to\infty$:

- $\frac{n}{2^n}\to 0$ (exponential growth dominates polynomial).
- For the first term: the exponent $\sqrt{n}+1/2-n = -(n-\sqrt{n}-1/2)\to -\infty$, so $2^{\sqrt{n}+1/2-n}\to 0$ exponentially, while $n$ grows only polynomially. Hence $n\cdot 2^{\sqrt{n}+1/2-n}\to 0$.

**Lower bound.** Every summand is non-negative ($2^{f(k)}+2^{-f(k)}>0$), so $S_n\ge 0$.

**Conclusion.** By the squeeze theorem,
$$0 \le S_n \le n\cdot 2^{\sqrt{n}+1/2-n}+\frac{n}{2^n}\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0,$$

therefore
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{2^{f(k)}+2^{-f(k)}}{2^n}=\boxed{0}.$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
